@node Equivalence predicates
@section Equivalence predicates
@nodedescription Procedures that deal with equivalence.

@deffn procedure eqv? @pari{obj} @parii{obj}
The @code{eqv?} procedure defines a useful equivalence relation on
objects.  Briefly, it returns @code{#t} if @vari{obj} and @varii{obj}
are normally regarded as the same object.  This relation is left
slightly open to interpretation, but the following partial specification
of @code{eqv?} holds for all implementations of Scheme.

@lisp
(eqv? 'a 'a)                 @ok{#t}
(eqv? 'a 'b)                 @ok{#f}
(eqv? 2 2)                   @ok{#t}
(eqv? 2 2.0)                 @ok{#f}
(eqv? '() '())               @ok{#t}
(eqv? 100000000 100000000)   @ok{#t}
(eqv? 0.0 +nan.0)            @ok{#f}
(eqv? (cons 1 2) (cons 1 2)) @ok{#f}
(eqv? (lambda () 1)
      (lambda () 2))         @ok{#f}
(let ((p (lambda (x) x)))
  (eqv? p p))                @ok{#t}
(eqv? #f 'nil)               @ok{#f}
@end lisp

The following examples illustrate cases in which the above rules do not
fully specify the behavior of @code{eqv?}. All that can be said about
such cases is that the value returned by @code{eqv?} must be a boolean.

@lisp
(eqv? "" "")          @unspecified{unspecified}
(eqv? '#() '#())      @unspecified{unspecified}
(eqv? (lambda (x) x)  @unspecified{unspecified}
      (lambda (x) x)) @unspecified{unspecified}
(eqv? (lambda (x) x)  @unspecified{unspecified}
      (lambda (y) y)) @unspecified{unspecified}
(eqv? 1.0e0 1.0f0)    @unspecified{unspecified}
(eqv? +nan.0 +nan.0)  @unspecified{unspecified}
@end lisp

Note that @code{(eqv? 0.0 -0.0)} will return @code{#f} if negative zero
is distinguished, and @code{#t} if negative zero is not distinguished.

The next set of examples shows the use of @code{eqv?} with procedures
that have local state.  The @nobr{@code{gen-counter}} procedure must
return a distinct procedure every time, since each procedure has its own
internal counter.  The @nobr{@code{gen-loser}} procedure, however,
returns operationally equivalent procedures each time, since the local
state does not affect the value or side effects of the procedures.
However, @code{eqv?}  may or may not detect this equivalence.
@end deffn
